  "page1": {
    "title": "A War That Divided",
    "subtitle": "How one country became two — and why peace matters.",
    "body": "Long ago, Korea was one country. But in 1950, a sad war began. This war is the Korean War. One side pushed down from the north, and the other side fought back.\n\nSoldiers fell, and many homes burned. Families had to <mark>flee from danger</mark>. They walked far to find a safe place. Leaving home like this is called taking refuge. Parents and children lost each other on the road.\n\nAfter three years, the fighting stopped. But the war did not truly end. A line split the land into two parts. The north and the south became divided. Many families could not meet again.\n\nToday we still hope for peace. We learn this story to keep peace safe.",
    "illustration": "assets/illustrations/mars/2026-07/07.jpg",
    "illustration_caption": "Families walking south to find safety, with a line dividing the land into two parts."
  },
